Scope {#scope}
==============

This proposal defines the [=Fetch Server API=], a server-side HTTP API built on the Fetch Standard's {{Request}} and {{Response}} types. It specifies:

*   A {{ServerContext}} interface providing connection metadata, server capabilities, and lifecycle management for incoming HTTP requests.
*   A {{ConnectContext}} interface extending {{ServerContext}} for CONNECT and extended CONNECT tunnel protocols.
*   An [=exchange=] state model governing response commitment, and an [=error code=] registry mapping protocol-version-independent codes to HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 error signals.
*   A {{Tunnel}} interface for bidirectional communication over established HTTP tunnels, including the Capsule Protocol and HTTP Datagrams.
*   A {{WebTransportSession}} interface for multiplexed streams and datagrams over HTTP connections.
*   A handler object model with `fetch()` and `connect()` methods for processing requests and tunnels.
*   An optional infrastructure layer providing `serve()`, {{Server}}, {{Listener}}, and lifecycle management.

Conformance {#conformance}
==========================

This specification has two layers:

**Core** (handler model): The {{ServerContext}}, {{ConnectContext}}, handler object pattern, and the handler callback signatures. A conforming implementation MUST support this layer.

**Infrastructure** (server lifecycle): The `serve()` function, {{Server}}, {{Listener}}, {{Closeable}}, {{ListenOptions}}, {{ServerOptions}}, and {{TLSOptions}}. An implementation MAY support this layer. An implementation that manages server lifecycle externally (e.g., an edge runtime where binding, TLS, and connection management are handled by the platform outside the application) is not required to expose `serve()`, {{Server}}, or {{Listener}}. Such an implementation is conforming as long as it implements the core layer.

<div class="note">
Cloudflare Workers is an example of a runtime that would implement the core layer but not the infrastructure layer. The application exports a handler object; the platform handles everything else. Node.js and Deno are examples of runtimes that would implement both layers.
</div>

A conforming implementation shall also conform to [[!ECMASCRIPT]] and [[!WEBIDL]].

Support for the following features is OPTIONAL at both layers:

*   Priority
*   Informational responses
*   Extended CONNECT and tunnel protocols
*   WebTransport
*   HTTP Datagrams
*   HTTP/3 and QUIC

An implementation that does not support an optional feature MUST still expose the relevant interface members. A method whose effect the implementation cannot provide throws a `"NotSupportedError"` {{DOMException}}; promise-returning methods reject with the same error. Priority is the exception: it is advisory by design and degrades silently &mdash; {{ServerContext/clientPriority}} returns the defaults, assignments to {{ServerContext/serverPriority}} are accepted and ignored, and an {{ServerContext/onpriority}} callback is never invoked.

{{ServerContext/deny()}} and {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} are not optional. Every implementation can decline a request; the wire-level fidelity of [=error codes=] varies by protocol, and where a signal is unavailable the implementation uses the closest supported behavior (e.g., the HTTP/1.1 fallback in [[#servercontext-denial]]). Applications rely on {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} for cleanup and logging actions that must complete, so every implementation MUST honor it (subject to the time limit in [[#servercontext-lifecycle]]).

Normative references {#normative-references}
=============================================

The following documents are referred to in the text in such a way that some or all of their content constitutes requirements of this document.

<div data-fill-with="references"></div>

Terms and definitions {#terminology}
====================================

For the purposes of this document, the terms and definitions given in [[ECMASCRIPT]], the Fetch Standard [[FETCH]], the DOM Standard [[DOM]], and the following apply.

<h3 class="no-toc" id="term-fetch-server-api"><dfn>Fetch Server API</dfn></h3>

the server-side HTTP API defined by this specification

<h3 class="no-toc" id="term-web-interoperable-runtime"><dfn export>web-interoperable runtime</dfn></h3>

ECMAScript-based runtime environment as defined by WinterTC

<h3 class="no-toc" id="term-server"><dfn dfn-type="dfn" id="dfn-server">server</dfn></h3>

an entity that accepts HTTP connections, receives requests, and sends responses

<h3 class="no-toc" id="term-handler"><dfn>handler</dfn></h3>

a JavaScript function provided by the application that processes incoming requests or tunnel establishment attempts

<h3 class="no-toc" id="term-exchange"><dfn export>exchange</dfn></h3>

the server-side processing of a single incoming request or tunnel establishment attempt, from receipt until the response (or tunnel) completes and all lifecycle-extension promises settle

<h3 class="no-toc" id="term-tunnel"><dfn dfn-type="dfn" id="dfn-tunnel">tunnel</dfn></h3>

a long-lived bidirectional communication channel established through an HTTP connection via the CONNECT method or extended CONNECT

<h3 class="no-toc" id="term-connect-protocol"><dfn export>connect protocol</dfn></h3>

the value of the `:protocol` pseudo-header in an extended CONNECT request, identifying the protocol being tunneled (e.g., `"websocket"`, `"webtransport"`, `"connect-udp"`, `"connect-ip"`)

<h3 class="no-toc" id="term-protocol-marker"><dfn>protocol marker</dfn></h3>

a `Symbol.for('server.protocol')` property on a [=handler=] object with an integer version value, used by the runtime to distinguish this API from legacy invocation conventions


Web IDL definitions {#idl}
==========================

SocketAddress {#idl-socketaddress}
----------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary SocketAddress {
  DOMString address;
  unsigned short port;
  DOMString family;
};
</pre>

A {{SocketAddress}} represents a network endpoint. The {{SocketAddress/address}} member is the IP address as a string. The {{SocketAddress/family}} member indicates the address family: `"IPv4"` or `"IPv6"`.

RequestPriority {#idl-requestpriority}
--------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary RequestPriority {
  unsigned short urgency = 3;
  boolean incremental = false;
};
</pre>

A {{RequestPriority}} represents a priority signal per [RFC 9218](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9218). The {{RequestPriority/urgency}} member is an integer in the range 0&ndash;7, where 0 is the highest priority and 7 is the lowest. The default is 3. The {{RequestPriority/incremental}} member indicates whether incremental delivery is preferred.

Callback definitions {#idl-callbacks}
-------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
callback PriorityCallback = undefined (optional RequestPriority priority = {});

callback FetchHandler = any (ServerContext ctx);

callback ConnectHandler = any (ConnectContext ctx);

callback ErrorHandler = any (ServerContext ctx, any error);
</pre>

The {{FetchHandler}} callback receives a {{ServerContext}} and returns a {{Response}}, `undefined`, or a {{Promise}} resolving to one of those. The return type is specified as `any` because Web IDL cannot express `(Response or undefined or Promise<Response or undefined>)` as a callback return type.

The {{ConnectHandler}} callback receives a {{ConnectContext}} and returns a {{Response}}, `undefined`, or a {{Promise}} resolving to one of those.

The {{ErrorHandler}} callback receives the context of a failed [=exchange=] (a {{ServerContext}} or {{ConnectContext}}) and the thrown value, and returns a {{Response}}, `undefined`, or a {{Promise}} resolving to one of those. See [[#handler-error]].

Options dictionaries {#idl-options}
-----------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary WebSocketUpgradeInit {
  sequence&lt;DOMString> protocol;
};

dictionary TLSCertificate {
  (DOMString or BufferSource) cert;
  (DOMString or BufferSource) key;
};

callback SNICallback = any (DOMString hostname);

dictionary TLSOptions : TLSCertificate {
  sequence&lt;DOMString> alpn;
  (SNICallback or record&lt;DOMString, TLSCertificate>) sni;
};

dictionary QUICOptions {
};

dictionary TimeoutOptions {
  unsigned long long handshake;
  unsigned long long idle;
  unsigned long long headers;
  unsigned long long receive;
  unsigned long long send;
  unsigned long long tunnelIdle;
  unsigned long long waitUntil;
};

dictionary ServerOptions {
  unsigned short port;
  DOMString hostname;
  TLSOptions tls;
  (boolean or QUICOptions) quic = false;
  boolean autoContinue = true;
  TimeoutOptions timeouts;
  AbortSignal signal;
};

dictionary ListenOptions {
  unsigned short port = 0;
  DOMString hostname = "0.0.0.0";
  TLSOptions tls;
  (boolean or QUICOptions) quic;
  TimeoutOptions timeouts;
};

dictionary HandlerObject {
  required FetchHandler fetch;
  ConnectHandler connect;
  ErrorHandler error;
};
</pre>

The ServerContext interface {#idl-servercontext}
------------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=*]
interface ServerContext {
  [SameObject] readonly attribute Request request;

  readonly attribute SocketAddress remoteAddress;
  readonly attribute SocketAddress localAddress;
  readonly attribute DOMString alpnProtocol;
  readonly attribute boolean encrypted;
  readonly attribute DOMString? serverName;

  readonly attribute RequestPriority clientPriority;
  attribute RequestPriority? serverPriority;
  attribute PriorityCallback? onpriority;

  readonly attribute boolean autoContinue;
  undefined sendInformational(unsigned short status,
                              optional HeadersInit headers);

  undefined deny(optional any error);

  undefined waitUntil(Promise&lt;any> promise);
};
</pre>

The {{ServerContext}} interface is the server-side processing environment for an incoming HTTP request. It provides the {{Request}}, connection metadata, server capabilities, and lifecycle management.

The ConnectContext interface {#idl-connectcontext}
--------------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=*]
interface ConnectContext : ServerContext {
  readonly attribute DOMString? connectProtocol;

  Promise&lt;Tunnel> accept(optional ResponseInit init = {});

  object upgradeWebSocket(optional WebSocketUpgradeInit options = {});
  Promise&lt;WebTransportSession> upgradeWebTransport();
};
</pre>

The {{ConnectContext}} interface is the server-side processing environment for an incoming CONNECT or extended CONNECT request. It extends {{ServerContext}} with protocol identification and tunnel establishment capabilities.

The Tunnel interface {#idl-tunnel}
----------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=*]
interface Tunnel {
  readonly attribute ReadableStream readable;
  readonly attribute WritableStream writable;

  CapsuleStream capsules();

  DatagramStream datagrams();

  undefined close();
  undefined destroy(optional any error);
  readonly attribute Promise&lt;undefined> closed;
};
</pre>

The {{Tunnel}} interface represents an established [=tunnel=] through an HTTP connection. It supports `Symbol.asyncDispose`, enabling `await using` declarations; see [[#tunnel-closure]].

Note: {{Tunnel}} structurally satisfies {{ReadableWritablePair}} &mdash; like {{TransformStream}}, it declares `readable` and `writable` attributes rather than inheriting the dictionary (Web IDL does not permit an interface to inherit from a dictionary, and the attributes carry behavior a dictionary snapshot cannot: after {{Tunnel/capsules()}}, they throw). It may therefore be passed directly to {{ReadableStream/pipeThrough()}} and anywhere else a {{ReadableWritablePair}} is accepted; once {{Tunnel/capsules()}} has been called, such conversion throws an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}, correctly.

The CapsuleStream dictionary {#idl-capsulestream}
-------------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary CapsuleStream : ReadableWritablePair {};
</pre>

A {{CapsuleStream}} provides typed access to the Capsule Protocol on the CONNECT data stream. Its {{ReadableWritablePair/readable}} member yields {{Capsule}} objects; its {{ReadableWritablePair/writable}} member accepts {{Capsule}} objects.

Note: {{CapsuleStream}} and {{DatagramStream}} are dictionaries, not interfaces: they are plain data carriers with no behavior of their own, returned once and destructured by the caller. Because they inherit from the Streams Standard's {{ReadableWritablePair}}, they may be passed anywhere a {{ReadableWritablePair}} is accepted, including {{ReadableStream/pipeThrough()}}.

The Capsule dictionary {#idl-capsule}
-------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary Capsule {
  unsigned long long type;
  Uint8Array data;
};
</pre>

A {{Capsule}} represents a single typed capsule as defined in [RFC 9297](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9297).

The DatagramStream dictionary {#idl-datagramstream}
---------------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary DatagramStream : ReadableWritablePair {
  required boolean unreliable;
};
</pre>

A {{DatagramStream}} provides access to the HTTP Datagrams associated with a [=tunnel=]. Its {{ReadableWritablePair/readable}} member yields {{Uint8Array}} payloads; its {{ReadableWritablePair/writable}} member accepts {{Uint8Array}} payloads. The {{DatagramStream/unreliable}} member indicates whether datagrams are sent as unreliable QUIC DATAGRAM frames (`true`) or as reliable DATAGRAM capsules (`false`).

Note: WebTransport session datagrams do not use {{DatagramStream}}; they use the {{WebTransportDatagramDuplexStream}} interface defined by [[WEBTRANSPORT]]. See [[#webtransport-datagrams]].

The WebTransportSession interface {#idl-webtransportsession}
------------------------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=*]
interface WebTransportSession {
  readonly attribute Promise&lt;undefined> ready;
  readonly attribute WebTransportReliabilityMode reliability;

  readonly attribute WebTransportDatagramDuplexStream datagrams;

  Promise&lt;WebTransportBidirectionalStream> createBidirectionalStream(
      optional WebTransportSendStreamOptions options = {});
  /* a ReadableStream of WebTransportBidirectionalStream objects */
  readonly attribute ReadableStream incomingBidirectionalStreams;

  Promise&lt;WebTransportSendStream> createUnidirectionalStream(
      optional WebTransportSendStreamOptions options = {});
  /* a ReadableStream of WebTransportReceiveStream objects */
  readonly attribute ReadableStream incomingUnidirectionalStreams;

  undefined close(optional WebTransportCloseInfo closeInfo = {});
  readonly attribute Promise&lt;WebTransportCloseInfo> closed;
  readonly attribute Promise&lt;undefined> draining;

  /* server-side extension; not part of the WebTransport API */
  undefined destroy(optional any error);
};
</pre>

The {{WebTransportSession}} interface represents an established WebTransport session, providing multiplexed streams and datagrams over an HTTP connection. It is the server-side counterpart of the [[WEBTRANSPORT]] `WebTransport` interface, and strictly follows that specification's types: {{WebTransportDatagramDuplexStream}}, {{WebTransportBidirectionalStream}}, {{WebTransportSendStream}}, {{WebTransportReceiveStream}}, {{WebTransportSendStreamOptions}}, {{WebTransportCloseInfo}}, and {{WebTransportReliabilityMode}} are the interfaces, dictionaries, and enumerations defined by [[WEBTRANSPORT]]; this specification does not duplicate them. Each member of {{WebTransportSession}} has the semantics of the like-named member of the `WebTransport` interface, adapted to the server side of the session. The exceptions are explicitly labeled <dfn export>server-side extensions</dfn> &mdash; {{WebTransportSession/destroy()}} and `Symbol.asyncDispose` &mdash; which have no `WebTransport` counterpart and are defined by this specification ([[#webtransport-lifecycle]]). Should [[WEBTRANSPORT]] later define equivalent members, this specification defers to those definitions.

Note: {{WebTransportSession}} deliberately omits the client-only surface of the `WebTransport` interface (the constructor, connection options, certificate hashes, `getStats()`, and pooling-related members). Additional members may be adopted as the server-side profile matures.

The Closeable interface mixin {#idl-closeable}
----------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
interface mixin Closeable {
  attribute boolean busy;
  Promise&lt;undefined> close();
  undefined destroy(optional any error);
  readonly attribute Promise&lt;undefined> closed;
};
</pre>

The {{Closeable}} mixin provides a uniform lifecycle interface shared by {{Server}} and {{Listener}}. Implementations of {{Closeable}} should support `Symbol.asyncDispose`, which calls {{Closeable/close()}} and waits for the {{Closeable/closed}} promise.

The Listener interface {#idl-listener}
--------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=*]
interface Listener {
  readonly attribute SocketAddress address;
};
Listener includes Closeable;
</pre>

The {{Listener}} interface represents a single network binding. The {{Listener/address}} attribute returns the {{SocketAddress}} that this listener is bound to.

The Server interface {#idl-server}
----------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=*]
interface Server {
  Promise&lt;Listener> listen(optional ListenOptions options = {});
  iterable&lt;Listener>;
};
Server includes Closeable;
</pre>

The {{Server}} interface manages one or more listeners and dispatches incoming requests to handlers.

The serve() function {#idl-serve}
---------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=*]
namespace FetchServer {
  Server serve(HandlerObject handler, optional ServerOptions options = {});
};
</pre>

<div class="note">
The namespace `FetchServer` is used here for specification purposes. The actual module specifier is implementation-defined. Possible values include `"http"`, `"node:http"`, `"node:serve"`, or a WinterTC-standardized module name. The import surface is an open question; see [[#open-module-specifier]].
</div>


Exchange states {#exchange-states}
==================================

Each [=exchange=] is in exactly one of five states. The state determines which context operations are valid, and is the foundation for the error model in [[#errors]].

: <dfn export for="exchange">open</dfn>
:: Nothing has been sent to the client. The initial state.
: <dfn export for="exchange">informational</dfn>
:: At least one informational (1xx) response has been sent; no final response has begun.
: <dfn export for="exchange">committed</dfn>
:: A final response has begun: the handler returned a {{Response}}, or a tunnel was accepted via {{ConnectContext/accept()}}, {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}}, or {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebTransport()}}.
: <dfn export for="exchange">denied</dfn>
:: {{ServerContext/deny()}} was called. Terminal.
: <dfn export for="exchange">aborted</dfn>
:: The client reset the stream, or the connection was lost, before the exchange completed. Terminal. The exchange's <dfn export>abort reason</dfn> is a [=protocol error=] describing the cause ([[#errors-inbound]]).

An exchange is <dfn export>response-capable</dfn> while it is [=exchange/open=] or [=exchange/informational=].

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Operation</th><th>[=exchange/open=]</th><th>[=exchange/informational=]</th><th>[=exchange/committed=]</th><th>[=exchange/denied=]</th><th>[=exchange/aborted=]</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>{{ServerContext/sendInformational()}}</td>
      <td>Allowed &rarr; [=exchange/informational=]</td>
      <td>Allowed</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Discarded</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{ServerContext/deny()}}</td>
      <td>Allowed &rarr; [=exchange/denied=]</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Discarded</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{ServerContext/deny()}} with the [=fall-through sentinel=]</td>
      <td>Allowed (remains [=exchange/open=]; rerouted to `fetch()`)</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Discarded</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{ConnectContext/accept()}} and the upgrade methods</td>
      <td>Allowed &rarr; [=exchange/committed=]</td>
      <td>Allowed &rarr; [=exchange/committed=]</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Throws</td>
      <td>Fails with the [=abort reason=]</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

The two failure behaviors are deliberate:

*   **Deterministic misuse throws.** The [=exchange/informational=], [=exchange/committed=], and [=exchange/denied=] states are entered by the handler's own actions, so a violation is a programming error: the operation throws an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
*   **Races are inert.** The [=exchange/aborted=] state can be entered by the peer at any moment; a handler cannot reliably check-then-act against it. Synchronous operations are silently discarded rather than throwing. Promise-returning establishment methods reject with the [=abort reason=].

Note: {{ServerContext/deny()}} is restricted to [=exchange/open=] because its default signal (`ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_REJECTED`) is a guarantee to the client that no processing occurred and the request may be safely retried &mdash; even for non-idempotent methods. Once anything has been sent, that guarantee would be false. The state model makes it structurally impossible to send it untruthfully.

ServerContext {#sec-servercontext}
==================================

The request {#servercontext-request}
------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">request</dfn> attribute returns a standard {{Request}} as defined in the Fetch Standard.

The {{Request}} is constructed by the implementation from the incoming HTTP message. It has:

<ul>
<li>`method`: The HTTP method.
<li>`url`: The full request URL, reconstructed from the request target, `Host` header, and connection properties (scheme, port).
<li>`headers`: The request header fields.
<li>`body`: The request body as a {{ReadableStream}}, or `null`.
<li>`signal`: An {{AbortSignal}} that is aborted if the client disconnects or the connection is lost.
<li>`trailers`: A `Promise<Headers>` that resolves to the trailing header fields after the body is consumed.
</ul>
</div>

Because `ctx.request` is a standard {{Request}}, it can be passed directly to client-side `fetch()` for proxying:

```js
async fetch(ctx) {
  return fetch(ctx.request);
}
```

Connection metadata {#servercontext-connection}
------------------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">remoteAddress</dfn> attribute returns the {{SocketAddress}} of the remote peer. If the remote address is not available (e.g., the runtime abstracts it away), the implementation MAY return a {{SocketAddress}} with empty {{SocketAddress/address}} and `0` {{SocketAddress/port}}.
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">localAddress</dfn> attribute returns the {{SocketAddress}} of the local endpoint the connection arrived on, identifying the binding when a server listens on multiple addresses or ports. The same availability escape applies as for {{ServerContext/remoteAddress}}.
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">alpnProtocol</dfn> attribute returns the ALPN protocol identifier negotiated for this connection. Common values are `"http/1.1"`, `"h2"`, and `"h3"`. If ALPN was not negotiated (e.g., plaintext HTTP/1.1), the value is `"http/1.1"`.
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">encrypted</dfn> attribute returns `true` if the connection is protected by TLS (including QUIC, where TLS is intrinsic), and `false` otherwise. This distinguishes plaintext HTTP/1.1 from HTTP/1.1 over TLS without ALPN, which {{ServerContext/alpnProtocol}} alone cannot.
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">serverName</dfn> attribute returns the SNI server name from the TLS ClientHello, or `null` if the connection is not encrypted or the client did not send SNI. Unlike the `Host` header, this value is what TLS certificate selection was based on; the two can legitimately differ, which matters for multi-tenant routing.
</div>

Note: The {{ServerContext/alpnProtocol}} value identifies the HTTP version of the connection. Handlers generally should not branch on this value. It is provided for logging, debugging, and the rare case where application behavior legitimately depends on the transport.

Priority {#servercontext-priority}
-----------------------------------

[RFC 9218](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9218) defines the Extensible Prioritization Scheme with {{RequestPriority/urgency}} (0&ndash;7, default 3) and {{RequestPriority/incremental}} (boolean, default false). Priority signals flow in two directions &mdash; client to server and server to implementation &mdash; represented by two properties:

*   **{{ServerContext/clientPriority}}** (read-only) &mdash; what the client asked for.
*   **{{ServerContext/serverPriority}}** (read/write) &mdash; what the server decided.

### Client priority ### {#priority-client}

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">clientPriority</dfn> attribute returns the client's current priority signal, parsed from the `Priority` request header.

The {{ServerContext/clientPriority}} getter is live: it always reflects the most recent client signal. When the client sends a PRIORITY_UPDATE frame (HTTP/2 or HTTP/3), the implementation updates the value returned by {{ServerContext/clientPriority}} before invoking any registered callback.

If the request has no `Priority` header and no PRIORITY_UPDATE frame has been received, {{ServerContext/clientPriority}} returns the default values (`{ urgency: 3, incremental: false }`).
</div>

### Reprioritization ### {#priority-reprioritization}

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">onpriority</dfn> attribute holds the callback invoked when a PRIORITY_UPDATE frame is received for this request's stream, following the web platform's event-handler idiom: `ctx.onpriority = fn`. The callback receives a {{RequestPriority}} dictionary with the new values. The attribute defaults to `null`; because it is a plain attribute, at most one callback is registered at a time, assignment replaces the previous value, and assigning `null` removes it.

PRIORITY_UPDATE frames are hop-by-hop (HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 only) and may arrive at any time during the request lifecycle. If no callback is registered, PRIORITY_UPDATE frames still update {{ServerContext/clientPriority}} &mdash; the handler is simply not notified synchronously.

For HTTP/1.1, where PRIORITY_UPDATE frames do not exist, the callback is never invoked. The initial priority from the `Priority` header (if present) is still available via {{ServerContext/clientPriority}}.
</div>

### Server priority ### {#priority-server}

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">serverPriority</dfn> attribute is a read/write property that overrides the server-internal scheduling priority for this response's delivery. It defaults to `null`, meaning "use the client's priority signal."

When {{ServerContext/serverPriority}} is `null`, the implementation uses the client's priority signal ({{ServerContext/clientPriority}}) for scheduling. When {{ServerContext/serverPriority}} is set, the implementation uses the server's value instead, regardless of subsequent PRIORITY_UPDATE frames from the client. (The client's updates still appear in {{ServerContext/clientPriority}} and still trigger the {{ServerContext/onpriority}} callback &mdash; the server simply overrides the scheduling decision.)
</div>

{{ServerContext/serverPriority}} is purely about server-internal scheduling. It does NOT set the `Priority` response header &mdash; that is for signaling priority preferences to intermediaries and is set directly on the {{Response}}:

```js
ctx.serverPriority = { urgency: 1 };   // internal scheduling
return new Response(body, {
  headers: { 'Priority': 'u=1' },      // signal to intermediaries
});
```

These are intentionally separate concerns. A server may override internal scheduling without signaling intermediaries, or vice versa.

Informational responses {#servercontext-informational}
------------------------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ServerContext">sendInformational(status, headers)</dfn> method sends an informational (1xx) response to the client.

<ol>
<li>If the implementation does not support informational responses, throw a `"NotSupportedError"` {{DOMException}}.
<li>If |status| is `101`, or is not in the range 100&ndash;199 inclusive, throw a {{RangeError}}.
<li>If the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/committed=] or [=exchange/denied=], throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
<li>If the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/aborted=], return.
<li>If |status| is `100`:
    <ol>
    <li>If no [=pending 100 obligation=] exists, throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
    <li>Construct a headers object from |headers| if provided, send a `100 Continue` response with those headers, and clear the obligation. The exchange is now [=exchange/informational=].
    <li>Return.
    </ol>
<li>Construct a headers object from |headers| if provided.
<li>If {{ServerContext/autoContinue}} is `true` and a [=pending 100 obligation=] exists, send a `100 Continue` response and clear the obligation.
<li>Send the informational response with status |status| and the constructed headers. The exchange is now [=exchange/informational=].
</ol>
</div>

Status `101 (Switching Protocols)` is categorically rejected: protocol upgrades are handled through the `connect()` handler, where {{ConnectContext/accept()}} performs the handshake on the application's behalf ([[#connectcontext-upgrade]]). A `101` is also unrepresentable in HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 ([RFC 9113 Section 8.6](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9113#section-8.6)), so permitting it would leak an HTTP/1.1 wire artifact through the version-transparent handler model.

Notable informational status codes:

*   **100 Continue**: Indicates the server is willing to accept the request body. Sent by the implementation according to the rules below.
*   **103 Early Hints**: Provides header fields (typically `Link` headers) that the client can use to start preloading resources before the final response ([RFC 8297](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8297)).

An implementation that does not support informational responses throws a `"NotSupportedError"` {{DOMException}} rather than silently discarding the call: a swallowed 1xx would misrepresent observable protocol behavior that applications depend on, such as `Expect: 100-continue` flows and Early Hints preloading.

Note: The Fetch Standard's `onInformation` callback (in `fetch()` options) is the client-side counterpart: it receives informational responses. {{ServerContext/sendInformational()}} is the server-side counterpart: it sends them.

### 100 Continue ### {#servercontext-100-continue}

When a request arrives with `Expect: 100-continue`, the implementation records a <dfn export>pending 100 obligation</dfn> rather than responding immediately. Sending `100 Continue` on arrival would invite the request body before the handler has had the opportunity to {{ServerContext/deny()}} the exchange, defeating the purpose of the `Expect` mechanism.

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ServerContext">autoContinue</dfn> attribute returns `true` if the implementation resolves the [=pending 100 obligation=] automatically, and `false` if sending the `100 Continue` is the application's responsibility. In the infrastructure layer the value is configured via {{ServerOptions/autoContinue}} (default `true`). In runtimes without the infrastructure layer, the value is determined by the runtime &mdash; platform policy, a compatibility flag, or similar external mechanism &mdash; and this attribute is how the application discovers it.
</div>

An explicit `sendInformational(100)` call is valid if and only if a [=pending 100 obligation=] exists, in both modes; it sends the `100 Continue` (with the caller's headers) and clears the obligation. Calling it when no obligation exists &mdash; the request carried no `Expect: 100-continue`, or a `100 Continue` was already sent &mdash; throws an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}: an unsolicited or duplicate `100 Continue` is always a programming error. ([RFC 9110 Section 15.2.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-15.2.1) merely has clients discard such interim responses; this API rejects them loudly rather than emitting meaningless ones.)

The obligation otherwise resolves at the first of the following events:

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Event</th><th>{{ServerContext/autoContinue}} is `true`</th><th>{{ServerContext/autoContinue}} is `false`</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>The handler calls `sendInformational(100)`</td><td colspan=2>Send `100 Continue` with the caller's headers; clear the obligation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The handler sends another 1xx via {{ServerContext/sendInformational()}}</td><td>Send `100 Continue` first, then the requested response</td><td>Send only the requested response</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The handler begins reading the request body</td><td>Send `100 Continue` &mdash; the server is ready to receive</td><td>Nothing is sent</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The handler returns a final {{Response}} without reading the body</td><td colspan=2>Omit, per [RFC 9110 Section 10.1.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-10.1.1)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>{{ServerContext/deny()}} is called, or the exchange is [=exchange/aborted=]</td><td colspan=2>Discard</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

An automatically sent `100 Continue` transitions the exchange to [=exchange/informational=], exactly as an explicit informational response does: bytes have been sent, so {{ServerContext/deny()}} is no longer available.

Flushing the pending `100 Continue` ahead of any other explicit informational response keeps the client's view deterministic: a client waiting on its `Expect` never has to interpret some other 1xx as an implicit answer. Any 1xx from the handler means the exchange is proceeding, so the implementation confirms the body is welcome first.

Note: When {{ServerContext/autoContinue}} is `false`, reading the request body without having sent the `100 Continue` can stall: clients typically wait for the `100` before transmitting content, though [RFC 9110 Section 10.1.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-10.1.1) advises them not to wait indefinitely. Liveness is the application's responsibility. What manual mode buys is finer control &mdash; for example, sending `103 Early Hints` while authorization is still deciding, without committing to receive the body.

Denial {#servercontext-denial}
-------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ServerContext">deny(error)</dfn> method signals that the handler is declining to process the request. No {{Response}} is generated; the handler returns `undefined` (or nothing) after calling {{ServerContext/deny()}}.

<ol>
<li>If the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/aborted=], return.
<li>If the [=exchange=] is not [=exchange/open=], throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}} (see [[#exchange-states]]).
<li>If |error| is the [=fall-through sentinel=]:
    <ol>
    <li>If the exchange is not [=upgrade-originated=], throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
    <li>Mark the exchange as falling through to `fetch()`. The exchange remains [=exchange/open=] and nothing is sent. This context becomes inert: subsequent calls to {{ConnectContext/accept()}}, the upgrade methods, or {{ServerContext/deny()}} throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}. See [[#connectcontext-fallthrough]].
    <li>Return.
    </ol>
<li>Set the exchange state to [=exchange/denied=].
<li>Reset the stream, carrying the protocol error code selected by |error|:
    *   HTTP/2: `RST_STREAM`
    *   HTTP/3: `RESET_STREAM`
    *   HTTP/1.1: Implementation-defined (close the connection, or send a 503 response and close as a pragmatic fallback)
</ol>
</div>

{{ServerContext/deny()}} is terminal for the handler: the only valid completion after calling it is `undefined` (or a promise resolving to `undefined`). The handler may perform additional work before returning &mdash; the exchange is not complete until the handler's promise and all {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} promises settle &mdash; but no response is expected, and resolving with any other value is an invalid return ([[#handler-errors]]).

If |error| has a `.code` property whose value is an [=error code=] sendable via {{ServerContext/deny()}} (see [[#errors-codes]]), the implementation maps it to the corresponding wire-level code. When |error| is omitted, has no `.code`, or the code is unrecognized, the default is `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_REJECTED` (REFUSED_STREAM / H3_REQUEST_REJECTED). This is the correct default because {{ServerContext/deny()}} means the request was not processed, which is exactly the semantic REFUSED_STREAM was designed to express. A client receiving this signal knows the request can be safely retried &mdash; including non-idempotent methods like POST.

The `ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY` code is a connection-level signal. Unlike the other codes which reset a single stream, `ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY` instructs the implementation to:

1.  Refuse the current request (as with any {{ServerContext/deny()}} call).
2.  Send a GOAWAY frame on the underlying connection, indicating that no new streams will be accepted.
3.  Allow in-flight streams (requests already being processed by other handler invocations on the same connection) to complete normally.

This provides a per-request escape hatch for connection-level concerns &mdash; rate limiting, credential revocation, or misbehavior detection &mdash; without exposing a connection object to the handler.

For HTTP/1.1, `ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY` closes the connection after the current exchange. Since HTTP/1.1 connections are serial (ignoring pipelining), this is equivalent to closing the connection.

Note: Proactive GOAWAY (shutting down connections without a triggering request) is handled by {{Closeable/close()}} and {{Closeable/destroy()}} in the infrastructure layer, not by {{ServerContext/deny()}}. The {{ServerContext/deny()}} mechanism covers the reactive case where a handler decides during request processing that the connection should be closed.

Request lifecycle {#servercontext-lifecycle}
--------------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ServerContext">waitUntil(promise)</dfn> method extends the lifetime of the request processing beyond the return of the handler function. The [=server=] does not consider the request fully complete until all promises passed to {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} have settled.
</div>

This is analogous to `ExtendableEvent.waitUntil()` in [Service Workers](https://w3c.github.io/ServiceWorker/) and `ctx.waitUntil()` in Cloudflare Workers.

Unlike the optional features enumerated in [[#conformance]], {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} MUST be supported by every implementation: applications depend on it for cleanup and logging actions that must complete.

An implementation MAY impose an implementation-defined time limit on how long it will wait for outstanding {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} promises to settle. In the infrastructure layer, this limit is configurable via the {{TimeoutOptions/waitUntil}} member of {{TimeoutOptions}} ([[#serve-timeouts]]). Reaching the limit does not cancel the promises &mdash; the exchange simply stops waiting for them; see [[#serve-timeouts]].

If a promise passed to {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} rejects, the implementation reports the rejection in an implementation-defined manner (e.g., logging). The rejection does not affect the response and does not invoke the handler's `error()` method ([[#handler-error]]).


ConnectContext {#sec-connectcontext}
====================================

Protocol identification {#connectcontext-protocol}
---------------------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="ConnectContext">connectProtocol</dfn> attribute returns the value of the `:protocol` pseudo-header for extended CONNECT requests:

<ul>
<li>`"websocket"` &mdash; WebSocket over HTTP/2 or HTTP/3
<li>`"webtransport"` &mdash; WebTransport session
<li>`"connect-udp"` &mdash; UDP proxying
<li>`"connect-ip"` &mdash; IP proxying
<li>Any other registered or private-use protocol identifier
<li>`null` &mdash; Plain CONNECT (no `:protocol` pseudo-header; TCP tunneling)
</ul>
</div>

HTTP/1.1 upgrade normalization {#connectcontext-upgrade}
---------------------------------------------------------

HTTP/1.1 uses the `Upgrade` header field ([RFC 9110 Section 7.8](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-7.8)) rather than extended CONNECT. To provide a unified handler model, an implementation normalizes HTTP/1.1 upgrade requests into {{ConnectContext}} objects: when an HTTP/1.1 request is received with `Upgrade` and `Connection: Upgrade` header fields, the implementation constructs a {{ConnectContext}} with {{ConnectContext/connectProtocol}} set to the first protocol token of the `Upgrade` field value and the {{ServerContext/request}} preserving the original method, headers, and URL. Such an exchange is <dfn export>upgrade-originated</dfn>. (The full, preference-ordered token list remains available via the request's `Upgrade` header.)

For WebSocket (`GET` with `Upgrade: websocket`), this means WebSocket handling is in one place &mdash; the `connect()` handler &mdash; regardless of HTTP version.

On an [=upgrade-originated=] exchange, {{ConnectContext/accept()}} performs the HTTP/1.1 upgrade handshake: it sends a `101 (Switching Protocols)` response with an `Upgrade` header field echoing {{ConnectContext/connectProtocol}}, and the resulting {{Tunnel}}'s {{Tunnel/readable}} and {{Tunnel/writable}} expose the raw connection bytes. As with extended CONNECT, the application never sends the `101` itself; the handshake is the implementation's concern ([[#servercontext-informational]]).

### Routing ### {#connectcontext-upgrade-routing}

*   If a `connect()` handler is provided, [=upgrade-originated=] exchanges are routed to it. The handler completes the upgrade ({{ConnectContext/accept()}}, {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}}), refuses it (a non-2xx {{Response}}, or {{ServerContext/deny()}}), or declines only the upgrade offer via the [=fall-through sentinel=] ([[#connectcontext-fallthrough]]).
*   If no `connect()` handler is provided, the upgrade offer is ignored, as [RFC 9110 Section 7.8](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-7.8) permits: the request is routed to `fetch()` as an ordinary request. The `Upgrade` header remains visible there, but the handler cannot act on it.
*   An implementation MAY instead handle particular upgrade protocols itself, without routing them to the application.

Note: This differs from CONNECT and extended CONNECT, which have no ordinary-request interpretation; those are answered with 501 Not Implemented when no `connect()` handler is provided.

Accepting a tunnel {#connectcontext-accept}
-------------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ConnectContext">accept(init)</dfn> method accepts the CONNECT request and establishes a [=tunnel=]. It returns a `Promise<Tunnel>` that resolves when the tunnel is established.

The optional |init| parameter allows setting response headers on the success response.

Accepting commits the [=exchange=] ([=exchange/committed=]): subsequent calls to {{ConnectContext/accept()}}, the upgrade methods, or {{ServerContext/deny()}} throw an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}, per [[#exchange-states]]. If the exchange is [=exchange/aborted=], the returned promise rejects with the [=abort reason=]. {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}} and {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebTransport()}} are committing operations subject to the same rules.

On [=upgrade-originated=] exchanges, accepting performs the HTTP/1.1 upgrade handshake instead of a CONNECT success response; see [[#connectcontext-upgrade]].
</div>

WebSocket upgrade {#connectcontext-websocket}
---------------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ConnectContext">upgradeWebSocket(options)</dfn> method is a convenience for WebSocket tunnel establishment. It performs the WebSocket-specific handshake (including subprotocol negotiation if |options|.{{WebSocketUpgradeInit/protocol}} is provided) and returns a standard [`WebSocket`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/web-sockets.html#websocket) object already in the `OPEN` state.

If {{ConnectContext/connectProtocol}} is not `"websocket"`, this method throws an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
</div>

WebTransport upgrade {#connectcontext-webtransport}
---------------------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="ConnectContext">upgradeWebTransport()</dfn> method establishes a WebTransport session. It returns a `Promise<WebTransportSession>` that resolves when the session is established.

If {{ConnectContext/connectProtocol}} is not `"webtransport"`, this method throws an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
</div>

Denying a tunnel {#connectcontext-deny}
---------------------------------------

A `connect()` handler can refuse a tunnel outright in two ways:

**Protocol-level denial** via {{ServerContext/deny()}} (inherited from {{ServerContext}}). This resets the stream without sending an HTTP response:

```js
async connect(ctx) {
  ctx.deny();  // REFUSED_STREAM — client may retry
  return;
}
```

**Application-level denial** by returning a {{Response}}. This sends a standard HTTP error response:

```js
async connect(ctx) {
  return new Response(null, { status: 403 });
}
```

The choice depends on the intended signal: {{ServerContext/deny()}} says "I never processed this" (protocol-level); a {{Response}} says "I processed this and the answer is no" (application-level).

On [=upgrade-originated=] exchanges there is a third option: declining only the upgrade offer while letting the request proceed as ordinary HTTP. See [[#connectcontext-fallthrough]].

Upgrade fall-through {#connectcontext-fallthrough}
--------------------------------------------------

Calling {{ServerContext/deny()}} with the <dfn export>fall-through sentinel</dfn> &mdash; `Symbol.for('server.fallthrough')` &mdash; denies the [=tunnel=] without denying the carrying request. The upgrade offer is treated as ignored, and the request proceeds to `fetch()` as ordinary HTTP:

```js
async connect(ctx) {
  if (ctx.connectProtocol === 'websocket' && !wsEnabled) {
    ctx.deny(Symbol.for('server.fallthrough'));
    return;  // fetch() takes it from here
  }
  const ws = ctx.upgradeWebSocket();
  // ...
}
```

The sentinel is a registered symbol, following the same pattern as the [=protocol marker=]: it costs no allocation, cannot collide with an application's error objects, and is not an [=error code=] &mdash; nothing is sent on the wire.

The sentinel is valid only on [=upgrade-originated=] exchanges. An HTTP/1.1 request bearing `Upgrade` is an ordinary request carrying an opportunistic offer that a server may ignore ([RFC 9110 Section 7.8](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-7.8)); CONNECT and extended CONNECT requests have no such interpretation &mdash; the request *is* the tunnel &mdash; so the sentinel on those exchanges throws an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.

After the `connect()` invocation settles with `undefined`, the implementation invokes `fetch()` with a fresh {{ServerContext}} for the same [=exchange=]:

*   Nothing has been sent and the exchange is still [=exchange/open=], so the `fetch()` handler retains its full capabilities &mdash; including {{ServerContext/deny()}}, honestly, since no processing is visible to the client.
*   The {{ServerContext/request}} is the same {{Request}}, `Upgrade` header intact.
*   {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} registrations and any [=pending 100 obligation=] carry over.
*   `fetch()` has no fall-through of its own; the flow terminates there.

`fetch()` is not invoked until the `connect()` invocation settles, so it is in the application's interest to return promptly after calling {{ServerContext/deny()}} with the sentinel &mdash; {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} is the tool for anything slow. If the invocation rejects, or resolves with anything other than `undefined`, the fault takes precedence: the exchange is still [=response-capable=], so the standard fault path applies ([[#handler-errors]]) and `fetch()` is not invoked.


Tunnel {#sec-tunnel}
====================

A {{Tunnel}} represents an established [=tunnel=] through an HTTP connection. It provides three layers of communication corresponding to the layers defined in the [Capsule Protocol](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9297):

1.  Raw data stream: Bidirectional byte stream on the CONNECT data channel.
2.  Capsule Protocol: Typed TLV-framed messages for control signaling.
3.  HTTP Datagrams: Discrete messages that may be unreliable on HTTP/3.

Raw data stream {#tunnel-raw}
-----------------------------

The {{Tunnel/readable}} and {{Tunnel/writable}} attributes provide direct access to the CONNECT data stream as a {{ReadableStream}} and {{WritableStream}} of bytes.

For a plain CONNECT tunnel (no `:protocol`), the raw data stream carries the tunneled TCP payload. For WebSocket, the raw data stream carries WebSocket frames.

For protocols that use the Capsule Protocol, consuming the raw data stream directly and consuming capsules are mutually exclusive.

Capsule Protocol {#tunnel-capsules}
-----------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="Tunnel">capsules()</dfn> method returns a {{CapsuleStream}} providing typed access to the Capsule Protocol on the CONNECT data stream. Calling {{Tunnel/capsules()}} consumes the raw data stream &mdash; subsequent access to {{Tunnel/readable}} or {{Tunnel/writable}}, and any subsequent call to {{Tunnel/capsules()}} itself, throws an `"InvalidStateError"` {{DOMException}}.
</div>

```js
const { readable, writable } = tunnel.capsules();

// Reading capsules
for await (const capsule of readable) {
  console.log(capsule.type, capsule.data);
}

// Writing capsules
const writer = writable.getWriter();
await writer.write({ type: 0xff37a2, data: new Uint8Array([...]) });
```

HTTP Datagrams {#tunnel-datagrams}
----------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="Tunnel">datagrams()</dfn> method returns a {{DatagramStream}} providing access to HTTP Datagrams associated with this [=tunnel=]. The method is idempotent in effect: each call returns a new dictionary object containing the same underlying {{ReadableStream}} and {{WritableStream}} objects.

On HTTP/3 connections where QUIC DATAGRAM frames are available, datagrams are sent and received as unreliable QUIC DATAGRAM frames. The {{DatagramStream/unreliable}} member is `true`.

On HTTP/2 or HTTP/1.1 connections, datagrams are sent as DATAGRAM capsules (type `0x00`) on the data stream. The {{DatagramStream/unreliable}} member is `false`. Delivery is reliable (TCP guarantees it), but the API is the same.
</div>

Both [CONNECT-UDP](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9298) and [CONNECT-IP](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9484) use HTTP Datagrams for their data plane. The {{Tunnel/datagrams()}} API provides the foundation for both.

Closure {#tunnel-closure}
-------------------------

Tunnel closure is a property of the tunnel's two directions. Each direction completes independently through the Streams API, and the tunnel closes gracefully when both have completed &mdash; no call to {{Tunnel/close()}} is required.

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Stream event</th><th>Tunnel effect</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>{{Tunnel/writable}} is closed via the Streams API</td>
      <td>Local half-close: pending data is flushed, then the end of the send direction is signaled (END_STREAM on HTTP/2, stream FIN on HTTP/3, TCP half-close for HTTP/1.1 CONNECT)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>The peer half-closes its send direction</td>
      <td>Remote half-close: {{Tunnel/readable}} closes after delivering any remaining data</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Both directions have completed</td>
      <td>The tunnel is closed; {{Tunnel/closed}} resolves</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{Tunnel/writable}} is aborted, or {{Tunnel/readable}} is cancelled</td>
      <td>Abnormal termination: the stream is reset carrying the [=error code=] mapped from the abort or cancel reason, defaulting to `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR` ([[#errors-outbound]]); the other constituent stream errors; {{Tunnel/closed}} rejects</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>The peer resets the stream, or the connection is lost</td>
      <td>Abnormal termination: both constituent streams error with the [=protocol error=] ([[#errors-inbound]]); {{Tunnel/closed}} rejects</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Closure is wire-level, not consumption-level: the tunnel is closed once its send direction has been flushed and ended and the peer's end-of-stream has been received. {{Tunnel/closed}} can therefore resolve while {{Tunnel/readable}} still holds undrained data; the application may continue reading after closure.

Half-closes are graceful; aborts are terminal. A lone half-close leaves the opposite direction fully operational &mdash; a tunnel can finish sending while continuing to receive, and vice versa. Cancelling or aborting either constituent stream &mdash; or calling {{Tunnel/destroy()}} &mdash; terminates the entire tunnel.

When the application has switched to the Capsule Protocol via {{Tunnel/capsules()}}, the active {{CapsuleStream}} pair carries these semantics in place of {{Tunnel/readable}} and {{Tunnel/writable}}; closing the capsule writable ends the send direction after the final capsule. {{DatagramStream}} pairs do not participate in closure: datagrams have no end-of-stream concept, closing a datagram writable stops local sends without any wire signal, and tunnel closure closes (or errors) the datagram streams rather than the reverse.

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="Tunnel">close()</dfn> method closes the [=tunnel=] gracefully, expressed in stream terms: it closes {{Tunnel/writable}} (a local half-close). It does not cancel {{Tunnel/readable}} &mdash; graceful closure does not discard the peer's in-flight data. Full closure, and settlement of {{Tunnel/closed}}, arrives when the peer completes its direction. An application that does not want the remaining inbound data can cancel {{Tunnel/readable}} instead, at the cost of abnormal termination. A peer that never completes its direction is subject to the {{TimeoutOptions/tunnelIdle}} timeout ([[#serve-timeouts]]).
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="Tunnel">destroy(error)</dfn> method immediately terminates the [=tunnel=] without draining:

<ol>
<li>If the tunnel is already closed or has already terminated abnormally, return.
<li>Reset the stream, carrying the [=error code=] mapped from |error| per the tunnel abort mechanism ([[#errors-outbound]]); when |error| is omitted or carries no sendable code, `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR` is used.
<li>Error the active constituent pair (raw or {{CapsuleStream}}) and any {{DatagramStream}} streams; pending writes reject.
<li>Reject {{Tunnel/closed}} with |error| if provided, or with a [=protocol error=] whose code is `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR` otherwise.
</ol>

{{Tunnel/destroy()}} operates on the tunnel rather than through its streams: it remains effective when the constituent streams are locked (e.g., mid-pipe) or the raw pair has been superseded by {{Tunnel/capsules()}}. Called while a graceful {{Tunnel/close()}} is pending, it short-circuits the drain &mdash; destroy wins.
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="Tunnel">closed</dfn> attribute returns a promise that resolves when the tunnel is closed cleanly, and rejects with a [=protocol error=] when the tunnel terminates abnormally &mdash; for example, the peer reset the stream or the connection was lost (see [[#errors-inbound]]).
</div>

<div algorithm="Tunnel async disposal">
{{Tunnel}} supports `Symbol.asyncDispose`, enabling `await using` declarations. The disposal steps are:

<ol>
<li>If the tunnel is already closed or has already terminated abnormally, return a promise resolved with `undefined`. Disposal does not re-surface an error the application has already had the opportunity to observe via {{Tunnel/closed}}.
<li>Otherwise, call {{Tunnel/close()}} and return a promise that settles as closure completes: fulfilled on clean closure, or rejected with the [=protocol error=] if the tunnel terminates abnormally while closing.
</ol>
</div>

```js
async connect(ctx) {
  if (ctx.connectProtocol !== 'connect-udp') {
    return new Response(null, { status: 501 });
  }
  await using tunnel = await ctx.accept();
  await proxyUdpPayloads(tunnel.datagrams());
  // Scope exit closes the tunnel and awaits closure —
  // including on early return or throw.
}
```

Note: There is no `Symbol.dispose`: tunnel closure is inherently asynchronous, so {{Tunnel}} supports `await using` but not synchronous `using`.


WebTransport {#webtransport}
============================

WebTransport provides multiplexed streams and unreliable datagrams over an HTTP connection. On HTTP/3, it uses native QUIC streams and QUIC DATAGRAM frames. On HTTP/2, it falls back to the Capsule Protocol.

Session establishment {#webtransport-session}
---------------------------------------------

A {{WebTransportSession}} is obtained via {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebTransport()}}:

```js
async connect(ctx) {
  if (ctx.connectProtocol === 'webtransport') {
    const session = await ctx.upgradeWebTransport();

    // Accept incoming bidirectional streams
    for await (const stream of session.incomingBidirectionalStreams) {
      handleStream(stream);  // { readable, writable }
    }
  }
}
```

Streams {#webtransport-streams}
-------------------------------

A {{WebTransportSession}} can carry multiple independent streams, using the stream types defined by [[WEBTRANSPORT]]:

*   Bidirectional streams: Opened by either side. Each is a {{WebTransportBidirectionalStream}} whose `readable` is a {{WebTransportReceiveStream}} and whose `writable` is a {{WebTransportSendStream}}.
*   Unidirectional streams: Opened by one side, read by the other, as a {{WebTransportSendStream}} or {{WebTransportReceiveStream}} respectively.

On HTTP/3, each WebTransport stream maps to a native QUIC stream. Streams are independent: a slow stream does not block others.

On HTTP/2, streams are multiplexed over the single CONNECT data stream using the Capsule Protocol. Head-of-line blocking applies (TCP guarantees ordering).

Datagrams {#webtransport-datagrams}
-----------------------------------

The {{WebTransportSession/datagrams}} attribute is a {{WebTransportDatagramDuplexStream}} as defined by [[WEBTRANSPORT]]: incoming datagrams arrive on its `readable`, and outgoing datagram writables are created with `createWritable()`.

Note: This deliberately differs from a [=tunnel=]'s {{Tunnel/datagrams()}}, which returns the simpler {{DatagramStream}} pair. The WebTransport datagram surface carries session-level controls (maximum datagram size, expiration ages, buffer depths) and send-ordering machinery that [[WEBTRANSPORT]] defines and this specification does not duplicate.

Reliability {#webtransport-reliability}
---------------------------------------

The {{WebTransportSession/reliability}} attribute (a {{WebTransportReliabilityMode}}, per [[WEBTRANSPORT]]) reflects the underlying transport mechanism:

*   `"supports-unreliable"`: Native QUIC streams and QUIC DATAGRAM frames (HTTP/3). No head-of-line blocking between streams. Datagrams may be dropped.
*   `"reliable-only"`: Capsule Protocol over a single HTTP/2 data stream. Head-of-line blocking applies. Datagrams are delivered reliably as DATAGRAM capsules.

Because {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebTransport()}} resolves only once the session is established, the `"pending"` value is never observed on a {{WebTransportSession}}.

Lifecycle {#webtransport-lifecycle}
-----------------------------------

Two [=server-side extensions=] supplement the [[WEBTRANSPORT]] lifecycle members, mirroring {{Tunnel}}.

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="WebTransportSession">destroy(error)</dfn> method immediately terminates the session without a clean close signal:

<ol>
<li>If the session is already closed or has already terminated abnormally, return.
<li>Reset the session's underlying CONNECT stream, carrying the [=error code=] mapped from |error| per the tunnel abort mechanism ([[#errors-outbound]]); when |error| is omitted or carries no sendable code, `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR` is used. No clean session-close signal is sent.
<li>Error all of the session's streams &mdash; bidirectional, unidirectional, and datagram &mdash; and reject any pending stream-creation promises.
<li>Reject {{WebTransportSession/closed}} with |error| if provided, or with a [=protocol error=] whose code is `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR` otherwise.
</ol>

Where {{WebTransportSession/close()}} performs the clean termination defined by [[WEBTRANSPORT]] &mdash; the peer's `closed` promise resolves with the {{WebTransportCloseInfo}} &mdash; {{WebTransportSession/destroy()}} is the abrupt one: the peer observes abnormal session termination, which the WebTransport API already defines as a `closed` rejection. Like {{Tunnel/destroy()}}, it operates on the session rather than through its streams, remains effective when streams are locked, and short-circuits a pending clean close.
</div>

{{WebTransportSession}} supports `Symbol.asyncDispose`, enabling `await using` declarations, with the same disposal steps as {{Tunnel}}: if the session is already closed or has terminated abnormally, the disposal promise resolves with `undefined`; otherwise {{WebTransportSession/close()}} is called with a default {{WebTransportCloseInfo}} and the promise settles as termination completes &mdash; fulfilled on clean closure, rejected if termination goes abnormal while closing. As with {{Tunnel}}, there is no `Symbol.dispose`.

```js
async connect(ctx) {
  if (ctx.connectProtocol !== 'webtransport') {
    return new Response(null, { status: 501 });
  }
  await using session = await ctx.upgradeWebTransport();
  for await (const stream of session.incomingBidirectionalStreams) {
    handleStream(stream);
  }
  // Scope exit performs a clean close and awaits termination.
}
```


Errors {#errors}
================

HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 carry typed error signals in both directions: stream resets (`RST_STREAM`, `RESET_STREAM`, `STOP_SENDING`) and connection-level `GOAWAY` frames, each with an error code. This section defines a protocol-version-independent registry of [=error codes=], the shape of the JavaScript errors that carry them, and how they propagate outbound (server to client) and inbound (client to server).

Error codes {#errors-codes}
---------------------------

An <dfn export>error code</dfn> is a string identifying a protocol-level error condition independently of HTTP version.

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Code</th><th>HTTP/2</th><th>HTTP/3</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_REJECTED`</dfn></td>
      <td>REFUSED_STREAM</td>
      <td>H3_REQUEST_REJECTED</td>
      <td>Not processed. The client may safely retry, including non-idempotent methods.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED`</dfn></td>
      <td>CANCEL</td>
      <td>H3_REQUEST_CANCELLED</td>
      <td>Intentionally cancelled. May have been partially processed.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR`</dfn></td>
      <td>INTERNAL_ERROR</td>
      <td>H3_INTERNAL_ERROR</td>
      <td>Internal error in the HTTP stack or application.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_STREAM_RESET`</dfn></td>
      <td>any other code</td>
      <td>any other code</td>
      <td>Generic stream reset; the wire code has no more specific mapping.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY`</dfn></td>
      <td>GOAWAY frame</td>
      <td>GOAWAY frame</td>
      <td>The connection is winding down; no new streams will be accepted.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_PROTOCOL_ERROR`</dfn></td>
      <td>PROTOCOL_ERROR</td>
      <td>H3_GENERAL_PROTOCOL_ERROR</td>
      <td>Framing or protocol violation.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR`</dfn></td>
      <td>CONNECT_ERROR</td>
      <td>H3_CONNECT_ERROR</td>
      <td>A tunnel was reset or abnormally closed.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_BODY_REJECTED`</dfn></td>
      <td>RST_STREAM (NO_ERROR) after a complete response</td>
      <td>STOP_SENDING (H3_NO_ERROR)</td>
      <td>The server does not want the remainder of the request body; the response is unaffected.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_CONNECTION_RESET`</dfn></td>
      <td colspan=2>transport-level</td>
      <td>The underlying connection was lost.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn export>`ERR_HTTP_TIMEOUT`</dfn></td>
      <td colspan=2>transport-level</td>
      <td>An implementation-defined or configured ([[#serve-timeouts]]) timeout elapsed.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Where each code may appear:

*   Sendable via {{ServerContext/deny()}}: `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_REJECTED` (the default), `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED`, `ERR_HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR`, `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR`, `ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY`.
*   Sendable by aborting a committed response ([[#errors-outbound]]): `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED`, `ERR_HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR` (the default).
*   Sendable by cancelling the request body ([[#errors-outbound]]): `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_BODY_REJECTED`.
*   Sendable by aborting a [=tunnel=]'s constituent streams ([[#errors-outbound]]): `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED`, `ERR_HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR`, `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR` (the default).
*   Observable inbound ([[#errors-inbound]]): `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED`, `ERR_HTTP_STREAM_RESET`, `ERR_HTTP_PROTOCOL_ERROR`, `ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY`, `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR`, `ERR_HTTP_CONNECTION_RESET`, `ERR_HTTP_TIMEOUT`.

Note: The registry is deliberately small. Most HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 error codes (`FLOW_CONTROL_ERROR`, `FRAME_SIZE_ERROR`, ...) are protocol plumbing that applications cannot act on; unrecognized wire codes surface as `ERR_HTTP_STREAM_RESET`. The taxonomy is designed to be shared with client-side `fetch()` error reporting, where the same codes describe the same wire events observed from the other end.

Protocol error objects {#errors-objects}
----------------------------------------

A <dfn export>protocol error</dfn> is a {{TypeError}} with:

*   a `code` own property whose value is an [=error code=]; and
*   optionally, a `detail` own property whose value is an object of the form `{ protocol, errorCode, errorName }` carrying the raw wire-level information (e.g., `{ protocol: "h2", errorCode: 0x07, errorName: "REFUSED_STREAM" }`).

The `cause` property is not used for protocol metadata; it retains its conventional role of chaining an underlying `Error`.

Note: Constructing errors with a `code` option depends on the [TC39 Error Code proposal](https://github.com/tc39/proposal-error-code-property), which adds a standardized `code` property to the `Error` constructor options. If that proposal does not advance, `code` remains an own property assigned by the implementation; the observable shape is the same.

Outbound errors {#errors-outbound}
----------------------------------

A handler signals protocol-level errors to the client through four mechanisms:

1.  **Before commitment**: {{ServerContext/deny()}} resets the stream. Valid only while the exchange is [=exchange/open=]. See [[#servercontext-denial]].

2.  **After commitment**: if the {{ReadableStream}} serving as the {{Response}} body errors, the implementation resets the stream. If the erroring value is a [=protocol error=] with a sendable code, that code is mapped to the wire; otherwise `ERR_HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR` is used. `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_REJECTED` is not sendable here: its retryability guarantee cannot be honored once bytes have been sent, and the state model keeps it structurally unreachable after commitment.

3.  **Request body refusal**: cancelling the request body's {{ReadableStream}} signals that the server does not want the remainder of the upload &mdash; `STOP_SENDING` on HTTP/3, or `RST_STREAM` with NO_ERROR after a complete response on HTTP/2, per [RFC 9113 Section 8.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9113#section-8.1). The response side of the exchange is unaffected. This is the outbound use of `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_BODY_REJECTED`.

4.  **Tunnel abort**: aborting either constituent stream of an established [=tunnel=] &mdash; its writable via abort, or its readable via cancel &mdash; or calling {{Tunnel/destroy()}} terminates the tunnel abnormally and resets the stream. If the abort, cancel, or destroy reason is a [=protocol error=] with a sendable code, that code is mapped to the wire; otherwise `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR` is used. See [[#tunnel-closure]]. {{WebTransportSession/destroy()}} terminates a WebTransport session the same way, by resetting its CONNECT stream ([[#webtransport-lifecycle]]).

Inbound errors {#errors-inbound}
--------------------------------

When the client resets the request stream or the connection is lost, the [=exchange=] becomes [=exchange/aborted=] and the event propagates as a [=protocol error=] &mdash; the exchange's [=abort reason=] &mdash; on every surface the handler may be observing:

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Surface</th><th>Propagation</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>`request.signal`</td><td>Aborted, with the protocol error as the abort reason</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Reading `request.body`</td><td>The read rejects with the protocol error</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A streaming {{Response}} body</td><td>The {{ReadableStream}} is cancelled with the protocol error as the reason</td></tr>
    <tr><td>{{Tunnel/closed}} / {{WebTransportSession/closed}}</td><td>Rejects with the protocol error on abnormal termination</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A [=tunnel=]'s constituent streams</td><td>The readable errors, and pending or subsequent writes to the writable reject, with the protocol error</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pending {{ConnectContext/accept()}} or upgrade promises</td><td>Reject with the protocol error</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

The code distinguishes situations a handler may reasonably treat differently: `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_CANCELLED` (the client deliberately cancelled), `ERR_HTTP_CONNECTION_RESET` (the connection died), `ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY` (the exchange could not complete before an orderly connection wind-down). Inbound wire codes with no specific mapping surface as `ERR_HTTP_STREAM_RESET`.

Handler model {#handler-model}
==============================

An application provides one or two handler functions: `fetch()` for standard request/response exchanges, and optionally `connect()` for tunnel protocols.

The FetchHandler {#handler-fetch}
---------------------------------

The `fetch()` [=handler=] receives a {{ServerContext}} and returns a {{Response}}, `undefined`, or a {{Promise}} resolving to one.

Returning `undefined` is valid only after calling {{ServerContext/deny()}}, or when the [=exchange=] was [=exchange/aborted=]. Any other `undefined` &mdash; or any return value that is not a {{Response}} &mdash; is a programming error: the implementation invokes the `error()` handler ([[#handler-error]]) with a {{TypeError}} describing the invalid return, falling back to 500 Internal Server Error.

```js
async fetch(ctx) {
  const { request } = ctx;
  const url = new URL(request.url);

  // Deny under load
  if (atCapacity) {
    ctx.deny();
    return;
  }

  if (url.pathname === '/api/data') {
    return Response.json({ ok: true });
  }

  return new Response("Not Found", { status: 404 });
}
```

The ConnectHandler {#handler-connect}
-------------------------------------

The `connect()` [=handler=] receives a {{ConnectContext}} and:

*   Returns `undefined` if the tunnel has been accepted, denied via {{ServerContext/deny()}}, or fallen through via the [=fall-through sentinel=].
*   Returns a {{Response}} to deny the request at the application level.
*   Throws or returns a rejected promise, causing a 502 Bad Gateway response.

```js
async connect(ctx) {
  switch (ctx.connectProtocol) {
    case 'websocket': {
      const ws = ctx.upgradeWebSocket();
      ws.addEventListener('message', e => ws.send(`Echo: ${e.data}`));
      return;
    }
    case 'webtransport': {
      const session = await ctx.upgradeWebTransport();
      handleWebTransport(session);
      return;
    }
    case 'connect-udp': {
      const tunnel = await ctx.accept();
      const dg = tunnel.datagrams();
      pipeUdpPayloads(dg);
      return;
    }
    default:
      return new Response(null, { status: 501 });
  }
}
```

If no `connect()` handler is provided, the implementation responds to CONNECT requests with 501 Not Implemented.

The ErrorHandler {#handler-error}
---------------------------------

The optional `error()` [=handler=] customizes the response when another handler faults. It receives the same context object the faulting handler received and the thrown value, and may:

*   return a {{Response}} (or a {{Promise}} resolving to one), which is sent;
*   call {{ServerContext/deny()}} (valid only while the [=exchange=] is [=exchange/open=]) and return `undefined`; or
*   return `undefined`, falling through to the default: 500 Internal Server Error for `fetch()`, 502 Bad Gateway for `connect()`.

```js
export default {
  [Symbol.for('server.protocol')]: 1,

  async fetch(ctx) {
    return handle(ctx.request);
  },

  error(ctx, err) {
    if (err.code === 'ERR_AT_CAPACITY') {
      ctx.deny();  // REFUSED_STREAM — client may retry elsewhere
      return;
    }
    return new Response('Internal Error', { status: 500 });
  },
};
```

`error()` is invoked only while the exchange is [=response-capable=]: a `fetch()` or `connect()` handler threw, returned a rejected promise, or returned an invalid value before committing. It is not invoked for:

*   faults after commitment &mdash; the response or tunnel is aborted instead ([[#errors-outbound]]);
*   exchanges already [=exchange/denied=] or [=exchange/aborted=] &mdash; there is nothing to respond to;
*   rejected {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} promises &mdash; those are reported by the implementation;
*   faults in `error()` itself &mdash; there is no re-entry; the default response is sent.

Error handling {#handler-errors}
--------------------------------

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Condition</th><th>Behavior</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Handler calls {{ServerContext/deny()}}, returns `undefined`</td><td>Stream reset (per [=error code=])</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Handler calls {{ServerContext/deny()}} with `ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY`</td><td>Stream reset + GOAWAY on the connection</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Handler resolves with a non-`undefined` value after {{ServerContext/deny()}}</td><td>Invalid return: reported; nothing is sent; `error()` not invoked</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`connect()` calls {{ServerContext/deny()}} with the [=fall-through sentinel=], settles with `undefined`</td><td>`fetch()` is invoked for the same exchange</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`connect()` rejects or resolves with a non-`undefined` value after the [=fall-through sentinel=]</td><td>`error()` is invoked; fallback 502; `fetch()` not invoked</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`fetch()` returns a {{Response}}</td><td>That response is sent</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`fetch()` throws or rejects while [=response-capable=]</td><td>`error()` is invoked; fallback 500</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`fetch()` returns an invalid value</td><td>`error()` is invoked with a {{TypeError}}; fallback 500</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`fetch()` throws or rejects after commitment</td><td>Response stream aborted; `ERR_HTTP_INTERNAL_ERROR`</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`connect()` returns a {{Response}} before accepting</td><td>That response is sent</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`connect()` returns `undefined` after accept or deny</td><td>Normal completion</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`connect()` throws or rejects while [=response-capable=]</td><td>`error()` is invoked; fallback 502</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`connect()` throws or rejects after accepting</td><td>Tunnel aborted; `ERR_HTTP_CONNECT_ERROR`</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`error()` throws, rejects, or returns an invalid value</td><td>Default 500/502; no re-entry</td></tr>
    <tr><td>[=Exchange=] is [=exchange/aborted=] (client gone)</td><td>Handler outcome discarded; `error()` not invoked</td></tr>
    <tr><td>{{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} promise rejects</td><td>Reported by the implementation; response unaffected</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

The handler object {#handler-object}
-------------------------------------

Handlers are provided as an object with `fetch` and/or `connect` methods:

```js
const handler = {
  [Symbol.for('server.protocol')]: 1,

  fetch(ctx) {
    return new Response("Hello");
  },
  connect(ctx) {
    // ...
  },
};

serve(handler, { port: 8080 });
```

When the handler is an object with methods, `this` inside the handler refers to the handler object. This allows the handler object to carry application state:

```js
const app = {
  [Symbol.for('server.protocol')]: 1,
  db: createPool(process.env.DATABASE_URL),

  async fetch(ctx) {
    const rows = await this.db.query('SELECT * FROM users');
    return Response.json(rows);
  },

  async [Symbol.asyncDispose]() {
    await this.db.end();
  },
};

serve(app, { port: 443, tls: { cert, key } });
```

Declarative export and protocol identification {#handler-declarative}
---------------------------------------------------------------------

As an alternative to the imperative `serve()` call, an application may export a default handler object. Because existing runtimes already use `export default { fetch() {} }` with different handler signatures (e.g., Cloudflare Workers passes `(Request, Env, ExecutionContext)`, Deno passes `(Request, ServeHandlerInfo)`), a handler object includes a [=protocol marker=] so the runtime can distinguish this API from legacy invocation conventions.

The marker is a `Symbol.for('server.protocol')` property with an integer version value:

```js
export default {
  [Symbol.for('server.protocol')]: 1,

  async fetch(ctx) {
    return new Response("Hello");
  },

  async connect(ctx) {
    if (ctx.connectProtocol === 'websocket') {
      const ws = ctx.upgradeWebSocket();
      ws.addEventListener('message', e => ws.send(e.data));
      return;
    }
    return new Response(null, { status: 501 });
  },
};
```

A conforming runtime that supports declarative export checks for the presence and value of `Symbol.for('server.protocol')` on the default export before invoking handler methods:

*   If `Symbol.for('server.protocol')` is present and its value is `1`, the runtime invokes `fetch()` with a {{ServerContext}} and `connect()` with a {{ConnectContext}}.
*   If `Symbol.for('server.protocol')` is absent, the runtime invokes handler methods using its existing (legacy) calling convention. Existing application code continues to work without modification.
*   If `Symbol.for('server.protocol')` is present but its value is not a recognized version, the runtime rejects the handler with a descriptive error.

The version number allows the protocol to evolve. This specification defines version `1`. Future revisions that change handler signatures would increment the version.

The **handler object** is the portable unit. The same handler object works with both patterns:

```js
const handler = {
  [Symbol.for('server.protocol')]: 1,
  fetch(ctx) { return new Response("Hello"); },
};

// In Workers or similar edge runtime:
export default handler;

// In Node.js, Deno, or Bun:
serve(handler, { port: 443, tls: { cert, key } });
```


Server configuration {#server-configuration}
=============================================

The serve() function {#serve-function}
--------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="FetchServer">serve(handler, options)</dfn> function creates a {{Server}}. If {{ServerOptions/hostname}} and {{ServerOptions/port}} (or {{ServerOptions/signal}}) are provided in the |options|, the server begins listening immediately. Otherwise, the server is created in an unbound state and must be explicitly started with {{Server/listen()}}.
</div>

```js
// One-step: create and listen
const server = serve({
  [Symbol.for('server.protocol')]: 1,
  fetch(ctx) {
    return new Response("Hello");
  },
}, {
  port: 443,
  hostname: '0.0.0.0',
  tls: {
    cert: readFileSync('cert.pem'),
    key: readFileSync('key.pem'),
  },
  quic: true,
});

// Two-step: create then listen
const server = serve({
  [Symbol.for('server.protocol')]: 1,
  fetch(ctx) {
    return new Response("Hello");
  },
}, {
  tls: { cert, key },
});

await server.listen({ port: 443, hostname: '0.0.0.0' });
```

ServerOptions {#serve-options}
------------------------------

When {{ServerOptions/port}} is `0`, the operating system assigns an available port. When {{ServerOptions/hostname}} is omitted but {{ServerOptions/port}} is provided, the default bind address is `"0.0.0.0"`.

The {{ServerOptions/autoContinue}} member controls automatic handling of `Expect: 100-continue` requests; see [[#servercontext-100-continue]]. The configured value is exposed to handlers via {{ServerContext/autoContinue}}.

TLS configuration {#serve-tls}
------------------------------

When {{ServerOptions/tls}} is provided, the server listens for TLS connections and negotiates ALPN. The default ALPN list is `["h2", "http/1.1"]`.

### SNI-based certificate selection ### {#serve-tls-sni}

The {{TLSOptions/sni}} option enables serving multiple hostnames with different certificates on the same listener. It can be either an object mapping hostnames to certificates or a callback function.

**Object form** &mdash; when the set of hostnames is known up front:

```js
serve(handler, {
  port: 443,
  tls: {
    cert: defaultCert,
    key: defaultKey,
    sni: {
      'example.com': { cert: exampleCert, key: exampleKey },
      '*.example.com': { cert: wildcardCert, key: wildcardKey },
      'other.net': { cert: otherCert, key: otherKey },
    },
  },
});
```

Keys support leading wildcard labels following [RFC 6125](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6125) Section 6.4.3 matching rules.

**Callback form** &mdash; for dynamic selection (ACME, vault lookup, etc.):

```js
serve(handler, {
  port: 443,
  tls: {
    cert: defaultCert,
    key: defaultKey,
    async sni(hostname) {
      const record = await certStore.lookup(hostname);
      if (record) return { cert: record.cert, key: record.key };
      return null;  // fall through to default
    },
  },
});
```

QUIC configuration {#serve-quic}
--------------------------------

When {{ServerOptions/quic}} is `true` or a {{QUICOptions}} object, the server additionally listens for QUIC connections on the same port and supports HTTP/3.

HTTP/3 requires TLS. If {{ServerOptions/quic}} is enabled and {{ServerOptions/tls}} is not provided, the implementation throws a {{TypeError}}. If {{ServerOptions/quic}} is enabled and the implementation does not support QUIC, it throws a `"NotSupportedError"` {{DOMException}} (see [[#conformance]]).

Timeouts {#serve-timeouts}
--------------------------

Activity timeouts are configured through the {{ServerOptions/timeouts}} member of {{ServerOptions}}. A {{ListenOptions/timeouts}} member on {{ListenOptions}} overrides the server-level configuration member-wise for that binding. All values are in milliseconds. An explicit `0` disables the timeout; an absent member leaves the value implementation-defined.

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Member</th><th>Scope</th><th>The clock runs while&hellip;</th><th>On expiry</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>{{TimeoutOptions/handshake}}</td>
      <td>connection</td>
      <td>TLS or connection setup is incomplete</td>
      <td>Close the connection</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{TimeoutOptions/idle}}</td>
      <td>connection</td>
      <td>No active [=exchanges=] exist and no frames are being exchanged</td>
      <td>GOAWAY and close (HTTP/1.1: close the keep-alive connection; QUIC: maps to the negotiated idle timeout)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{TimeoutOptions/headers}}</td>
      <td>exchange</td>
      <td>The request head is incomplete</td>
      <td>HTTP/1.1: 408 (Request Timeout) and close. HTTP/2 and HTTP/3: an implementation-defined stream or connection error &mdash; a complete head usually arrives in a single frame, so this is chiefly an HTTP/1.1 (slowloris) defense</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{TimeoutOptions/receive}}</td>
      <td>exchange</td>
      <td>The exchange is awaiting client data it needs &mdash; a body read is pending upstream</td>
      <td>Abort the exchange: [=exchange/aborted=], with a [=protocol error=] whose code is `ERR_HTTP_TIMEOUT` propagating through the surfaces in [[#errors-inbound]]</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{TimeoutOptions/send}}</td>
      <td>exchange</td>
      <td>Response data is queued and is not being accepted by the transport</td>
      <td>Same as {{TimeoutOptions/receive}}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{TimeoutOptions/tunnelIdle}}</td>
      <td>tunnel / session</td>
      <td>An established [=tunnel=] or {{WebTransportSession}} has no data flowing in either direction</td>
      <td>{{Tunnel/destroy()}} (or {{WebTransportSession/destroy()}}) with a [=protocol error=] whose code is `ERR_HTTP_TIMEOUT`</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{TimeoutOptions/waitUntil}}</td>
      <td>exchange</td>
      <td>{{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} promises remain unsettled after the response has completed</td>
      <td>The exchange stops waiting and completes; the promises are not cancelled (see below)</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Two principles govern every member:

*   **Activity, not duration.** Each clock measures the absence of progress and resets when progress occurs. No timeout in this specification caps the total duration of an exchange or tunnel &mdash; total-duration caps break streaming responses, large uploads, and long-lived tunnels by design. A consequence worth stating plainly: a slow <em>handler</em> never trips {{TimeoutOptions/receive}} or {{TimeoutOptions/send}}; those clocks run only while the peer owes progress. Defenses against deliberately slow trickle traffic (minimum-rate enforcement) are implementation latitude.

*   **Expiry is existing machinery.** Exchange-level expiry produces the [=exchange/aborted=] state with a typed [=protocol error=], flowing through the same surfaces as any inbound termination; tunnel expiry is {{Tunnel/destroy()}}; connection expiry is the GOAWAY path. The `error()` handler is never invoked for a timeout &mdash; timeouts are not handler faults.

On the wire, a stream reset caused by {{TimeoutOptions/receive}} or {{TimeoutOptions/send}} expiry carries CANCEL (HTTP/2) or H3_REQUEST_CANCELLED (HTTP/3), while the JavaScript-observable [=protocol error=] carries `ERR_HTTP_TIMEOUT`: the wire says the request is no longer wanted; the API says why.

Expiry of {{TimeoutOptions/waitUntil}} does not &mdash; and cannot &mdash; cancel the outstanding promises: JavaScript has no promise cancellation, and the work behind them is not aborted. Expiry means only that the [=exchange=] stops waiting for them: the exchange is considered complete, and resources the implementation associates with it may be torn down without further delay. The promises may still settle afterward; a late settlement has no effect on the exchange, and a late rejection is reported the same way as any {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} rejection ([[#servercontext-lifecycle]]). Whether the work itself continues to run after teardown depends on the runtime's execution model.

Implementations SHOULD enable {{TimeoutOptions/handshake}}, {{TimeoutOptions/idle}}, and {{TimeoutOptions/headers}} by default with implementation-defined values &mdash; they are connection hygiene. {{TimeoutOptions/tunnelIdle}} SHOULD be disabled by default: a quiet WebSocket or WebTransport session is legitimate, and keepalive traffic (ping/pong frames, session keepalives) counts as activity, so deployments that enable it compose with standard liveness practice.

Note: Runtimes that do not implement the infrastructure layer determine timeout behavior externally, as with the other members of {{ServerOptions}} (see [[#conformance]]).


Server and Listener {#server-listener}
======================================

{{Server}} and {{Listener}} share the {{Closeable}} interface for lifecycle management. On a {{Listener}}, these apply to a single binding. On a {{Server}}, they apply to all listeners collectively.

Binding and listeners {#server-binding}
---------------------------------------

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="Server">listen(options)</dfn> method binds the server to a network address and returns a `Promise<Listener>` that resolves when the binding is established.
</div>

{{Server/listen()}} may be called multiple times to bind the server to multiple addresses or ports:

```js
const server = serve(handler);
const http  = await server.listen({ port: 80 });
const https = await server.listen({ port: 443, tls: { cert, key } });
const quic  = await server.listen({ port: 443, tls: { cert, key }, quic: true });
```

{{Server}} is iterable over its active listeners:

```js
for (const listener of server) {
  console.log(listener.address);
}
```

### One-step vs. two-step vs. multi-listener ### {#server-binding-patterns}

```js
// One-step: serve() with port (single listener)
const server = serve(handler, { port: 8080 });

// Two-step: serve() then listen() (single listener)
const server = serve(handler);
await server.listen({ port: 8080 });

// Multi-listener: HTTP + HTTPS + HTTP/3
const server = serve(handler);
await server.listen({ port: 80 });
await server.listen({ port: 443, tls: { cert, key, sni } });
await server.listen({ port: 443, tls: { cert, key }, quic: true });
```

The Closeable interface {#sec-closeable}
-----------------------------------------

The {{Closeable}} mixin provides a uniform lifecycle interface shared by {{Server}} and {{Listener}}.

### busy ### {#closeable-busy}

<div algorithm>
When <dfn attribute for="Closeable">busy</dfn> is `true`, new [=exchanges=] are refused as if by {{ServerContext/deny()}} with no |error| argument: no handler is invoked, and the exchange is refused with the default [=error code=] &mdash; `ERR_HTTP_REQUEST_REJECTED`, i.e. REFUSED_STREAM on HTTP/2 and H3_REQUEST_REJECTED on HTTP/3. On HTTP/1.1, the implementation MAY respond with a 503 (Service Unavailable) &mdash; optionally carrying a `Retry-After` header field &mdash; without closing the connection, since the condition is temporary. In-flight exchanges continue to be processed, and outstanding {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} promises are unaffected.
</div>

The refusal applies uniformly to all exchange kinds &mdash; request/response, CONNECT, and [=upgrade-originated=] alike &mdash; since no handler runs to distinguish them. Because no handler is invoked, no context (and, per [[#why-context]], no {{Request}}) need ever be materialized for a refused exchange.

Setting {{Closeable/busy}} does not wind down connections: no GOAWAY is sent, and connections remain open so that requests are accepted again the moment the attribute returns to `false`. Connection wind-down is the domain of {{Closeable/close()}}, {{Closeable/destroy()}}, and per-request denial with `ERR_HTTP_GOAWAY`.

Note: {{Closeable/busy}} deliberately reuses {{ServerContext/deny()}}'s default semantics because they state exactly what a temporarily busy server wants to communicate: nothing was processed, and the client may safely retry &mdash; including non-idempotent requests. HTTP/2's ENHANCE_YOUR_CALM (0x0b) and HTTP/3's H3_EXCESSIVE_LOAD (0x0107) are deliberately not used: those codes signal that the <em>peer</em> is generating suspicious or excessive load &mdash; an accusation of client misbehavior, not a statement of the server's own unavailability.

Note: {{Closeable/busy}} is intended for brief back-pressure scenarios such as waiting for a downstream dependency to recover, performing a configuration reload, or coordinating with a load balancer during a rolling deploy. For permanent shutdown, use {{Closeable/close()}}.

### close() ### {#closeable-close}

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="Closeable">close()</dfn> method initiates graceful shutdown.

On a {{Listener}}, it stops accepting new connections, sends GOAWAY frames on HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 connections, allows in-flight requests to complete, and resolves when all connections are closed.

On a {{Server}}, it calls {{Closeable/close()}} on every active listener and waits for all {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} promises to settle.

Closing a listener does not close the server. Other listeners remain active.
</div>

### destroy() ### {#closeable-destroy}

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="Closeable">destroy(error)</dfn> method immediately terminates without draining. The {{Closeable/closed}} promise rejects with |error|, or with an `"AbortError"` {{DOMException}} when no |error| is provided: abrupt termination aborts in-flight work, and {{Closeable/closed}} must remain distinguishable from the graceful completion signaled by {{Closeable/close()}}.
</div>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Behavior</th><th>`close()`</th><th>`destroy()`</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>New connections</td><td>Refused</td><td>Refused</td></tr>
    <tr><td>GOAWAY sent</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><td>In-flight requests</td><td>Allowed to complete</td><td>Aborted immediately</td></tr>
    <tr><td>`waitUntil()` promises</td><td>Allowed to settle</td><td>Ignored</td></tr>
    <tr><td>{{Closeable/closed}} promise</td><td>Resolves</td><td>Rejects (destroy reason, or `"AbortError"`)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Returns</td><td>Promise (async)</td><td>undefined (sync)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

### closed ### {#closeable-closed}

<div algorithm>
The <dfn attribute for="Closeable">closed</dfn> attribute returns a promise that resolves when the server or listener is fully closed after a graceful {{Closeable/close()}}, and rejects &mdash; with the destroy reason, or an `"AbortError"` {{DOMException}} if none was given &mdash; when terminated via {{Closeable/destroy()}}.
</div>

### Symbol.asyncDispose ### {#closeable-dispose}

Both {{Server}} and {{Listener}} implement `Symbol.asyncDispose`, which calls {{Closeable/close()}} and waits for the {{Closeable/closed}} promise.

```js
{
  await using server = serve(handler, { port: 8080 });
  // Server is running
}
// Server has been gracefully closed
```

Signal-based termination {#server-signal}
-----------------------------------------

If an {{AbortSignal}} is provided in {{ServerOptions}}, aborting the signal triggers abrupt termination (equivalent to calling {{Closeable/destroy()}} with the signal's reason).

```js
const ac = new AbortController();
const server = serve(handler, { port: 8080, signal: ac.signal });

// Later:
ac.abort(new Error('shutting down'));  // Triggers destroy()
await server.closed.catch(() => {});
```

For graceful close, call {{Closeable/close()}} directly rather than using the signal.


HTTP version negotiation {#http-versions}
=========================================

Transparent protocol handling {#http-versions-transparent}
----------------------------------------------------------

A conforming implementation routes requests to the appropriate handler regardless of HTTP version. The `fetch()` handler receives all non-CONNECT requests. The `connect()` handler receives all CONNECT and extended CONNECT requests. The handler does not select which HTTP version to handle.

Feature availability by protocol version {#http-versions-features}
-------------------------------------------------------------------

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>HTTP/1.1</th><th>HTTP/2</th><th>HTTP/3</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Trailers (request)</td><td>Chunked TE only</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Trailers (response)</td><td>Chunked TE only</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Informational responses</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Extended CONNECT</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>WebSocket</td><td>Via Upgrade</td><td>Via ext. CONNECT</td><td>Via ext. CONNECT</td></tr>
    <tr><td>WebTransport</td><td>No</td><td>Capsule fallback</td><td>Native QUIC</td></tr>
    <tr><td>HTTP Datagrams (unreliable)</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (QUIC DG)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>HTTP Datagrams (reliable)</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (capsule)</td><td>Yes (capsule)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>CONNECT-UDP</td><td>No</td><td>Capsule fallback</td><td>Native QUIC DG</td></tr>
    <tr><td>CONNECT-IP</td><td>No</td><td>Capsule fallback</td><td>Native QUIC DG</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Full-duplex streaming</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>


Security considerations {#security}
====================================

*   **No CORS Enforcement**: This specification is for server-side runtimes. CORS is a browser security mechanism. Server-side implementations do not enforce CORS restrictions.

*   **Header Filtering**: Implementations should apply appropriate header filtering. Hop-by-hop headers are processed by the implementation and generally not exposed in {{Request}} headers. Trailer field filtering per [RFC 9110 Section 6.5.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-6.5.2) should remove fields not appropriate as trailers.

*   **Tunnel Security**: The `connect()` handler establishes tunnels that can carry arbitrary traffic. Applications must perform their own authorization before calling {{ConnectContext/accept()}}, {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebSocket()}}, or {{ConnectContext/upgradeWebTransport()}}.

*   **Denial of Service**: WebTransport sessions can open many streams; implementations should impose limits. {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}} extends request lifetime; implementations should impose timeouts. The Capsule Protocol and datagrams can generate high throughput; implementations should apply flow control.


Open issues {#open-issues}
==========================

Module specifier {#open-module-specifier}
-----------------------------------------

The import path for `serve()` is left implementation-defined. Possible values include:

*   `node:http` (extending the existing Node.js module)
*   `node:serve` (new Node.js module)
*   `http` (generic, non-Node.js-specific)

A standardized module specifier may be desirable if this API is adopted by WinterTC.

Structured fields on Headers {#open-structured-fields}
------------------------------------------------------

[RFC 9651](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9651) defines typed values (integers, booleans, tokens, byte sequences, etc.) for HTTP fields. The {{Headers}} interface exposes only raw strings. Adding structured field parsing to {{Headers}} (e.g., a `getStructured()` method) would benefit many use cases beyond priority.

This is a potential change to the Fetch Standard's {{Headers}} type and is out of scope for this specification, but would complement it.

Full-duplex streaming {#open-full-duplex}
-----------------------------------------

HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 support full-duplex streaming: the request body and response body are independent streams that can be read/written concurrently with independent half-close.

The Fetch Standard's `duplex` property currently allows only `"half"` for requests.

For server-side handlers, full-duplex is implicit: the handler can begin writing a response (via a {{ReadableStream}} body) while the request body is still being received.

Typed stream resets and error codes {#open-typed-resets}
-------------------------------------------------------

This specification defines the server-side half of typed error signaling: the [=error code=] registry, outbound signaling via {{ServerContext/deny()}} and stream aborts, and inbound propagation as [=protocol errors=] ([[#errors]]).

The client-side half remains open. When a `fetch()` call observes a stream reset, the Fetch Standard currently collapses it into a bare {{TypeError}}. Surfacing the same [=error codes=] there &mdash; on the rejection, and on errors of the response body stream &mdash; requires changes to the Fetch Standard's network error concept, and benefits from the [TC39 Error Code proposal](https://github.com/tc39/proposal-error-code-property) for a standardized `code` constructor option. The registry in [[#errors-codes]] is designed to be shared by both sides.

ServerResponse {#open-server-response}
--------------------------------------

This specification currently requires no server-specific response type. Handlers return a standard {{Response}} with trailer support provided by the Fetch Standard.

If future server-specific response capabilities are identified (e.g., server push, priority signaling, response-side lifecycle), a `ServerResponse` type may be introduced. The handler model is designed to accommodate this: the `fetch()` handler's return type can be extended to include `ServerResponse` without breaking existing handlers that return {{Response}}.


Addendum: Raw TCP sockets {#addendum-tcp}
=========================================

The handler object pattern is designed to be extensible. New handler methods can be added without changing existing signatures. This addendum sketches how the model extends to support raw TCP ingress &mdash; connections that are not HTTP.

Motivation {#tcp-motivation}
----------------------------

Some server-side runtimes (e.g., Cloudflare Workers) support arbitrary TCP ingress where non-HTTP connections are routed to the application. These connections carry raw bytes &mdash; no HTTP framing, no request method, no headers. They need a different handler and a different context.

SocketContext {#tcp-socketcontext}
----------------------------------

A `SocketContext` provides a WinterTC [Socket](https://sockets-api.proposal.wintertc.org/) and connection metadata. It is not related to {{ServerContext}} by inheritance &mdash; there is no HTTP {{Request}}, no {{ServerContext/sendInformational()}}, no {{ServerContext/deny()}}.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=*]
interface SocketContext {
  [SameObject] readonly attribute object socket;
  readonly attribute SocketAddress remoteAddress;
  readonly attribute DOMString alpnProtocol;
  readonly attribute DOMString? serverName;
  undefined waitUntil(Promise&lt;any> promise);
};
</pre>

*   {{SocketContext/socket}} &mdash; a `Socket` with `readable` and `writable` streams. Already connected; TLS (if any) is complete.
*   {{SocketContext/remoteAddress}} &mdash; the remote peer's {{SocketAddress}}.
*   {{SocketContext/alpnProtocol}} &mdash; ALPN protocol from TLS negotiation, or empty string. Can be any application-defined identifier, not just HTTP versions.
*   {{SocketContext/serverName}} &mdash; SNI hostname from TLS ClientHello, or `null`. Useful for multi-tenant routing.
*   {{SocketContext/waitUntil()}} &mdash; lifecycle extension, same as {{ServerContext/waitUntil()}}.

SocketHandler {#tcp-sockethandler}
----------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
callback SocketHandler = (undefined or Promise&lt;undefined>)
                         (SocketContext ctx);
</pre>

A `socket()` method is added to the handler object:

```js
export default {
  [Symbol.for('server.protocol')]: 1,
  fetch(ctx) { /* HTTP request/response */ },
  connect(ctx) { /* HTTP tunnels */ },
  socket(ctx) { /* Raw TCP connections */ },
};
```

Routing {#tcp-routing}
----------------------

How the implementation distinguishes HTTP from non-HTTP connections is implementation-defined. Common approaches include port-based routing, ALPN-based routing (a non-HTTP ALPN token), or protocol detection (inspecting the first bytes of the connection).

If no `socket()` handler is provided and a non-HTTP connection arrives, the implementation closes the connection. If the handler throws, the implementation closes the socket.
